          "text": "{\"target_identification\":{\"speaker\":\"Goneril\",\"addressee\":\"Lear\",\"anchor_quote\":\"Sir, I love you more than word can wield the matter;\\nDearer than eyesight, space, and liberty;\\nBeyond what can be valu’d, rich or rare;\\nNo less than life, with grace, health, beauty, honour;\\nAs much as child e’er lov’d, or father found;\\nA love that makes breath poor and speech unable;\\nBeyond all manner of so much I love you.\"},\"target_translation\":\"I love you.\",\"dimensions\":{\"T\":{\"score\":4,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.99,\"reason\":\"The focal expression directly presents Goneril's loving emotional state toward Lear as true. Its reporting or avowing force is clear regardless of whether the declaration is sincere.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"I love you\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Which of you shall we say doth love us most?\"}]},\"P\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.95,\"reason\":\"The focal expression avows present love but does not promise future conduct, renew an obligation, or undertake any specified commitment. Lear's subsequent grant responds to the declaration and wider speech; it is not uptake of a promise contained in these words.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"I love you\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Our eldest born, speak first.\"}]},\"E\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.93,\"reason\":\"This is a grammatically complete assertion delivered as the solicited opening of a formal answer, not a reflex-like or affective verbal discharge. The heightened language surrounding it does not by itself create expressive-discharge force.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Sir, I love you more than word can wield the matter;\"}]},\"O\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.88,\"reason\":\"The focal words function centrally as an avowal of love. Although they answer a competitive, reward-linked question and may have a persuasive purpose, the supplied evidence does not establish a separate core force beyond the T mode.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Which of you shall we say doth love us most?\\nThat we our largest bounty may extend\\nWhere nature doth with merit challenge.—Goneril,\\nOur eldest born, speak first.\"},{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"I love you\"}]}},\"original_speech_situation\":\"In a formal public proceeding, Lear asks his daughters to state which loves him most and explicitly connects their answers to the distribution of his bounty. Goneril, called upon first, begins an elaborate declaration with the focal avowal.\",\"affection_evidence\":\"mixed\",\"undertaking_and_uptake\":\"The focal expression contains no specified undertaking by Goneril. Lear subsequently grants Goneril and Albany territory after her full declaration, showing favorable reception of the avowal, but this is not acceptance of a promise. The passage alone does not settle the sincerity or actual degree of her affection; Cordelia's contrasting remarks cast some doubt on the sisters' expansive claims.\",\"recognised_work\":true,\"recognition_note\":\"The supplied passage is recognisable as the opening love-test scene of Shakespeare's King Lear. The assessment uses only the supplied text rather than additional plot knowledge.\",\"limitations\":\"Only the marked occurrence “I love you” is classified, though its immediate speech situation informs the analysis. The supplied passage does not conclusively establish Goneril's sincerity, private emotional state, or longer-term intentions, and no omitted context is assumed absent from the work.\"}"
